    "why_it_is_refuted": [
      "L3 and L4 have the SAME basin width of 4 across a 3.5x difference in solve rate (0.438 vs 0.125). If the basin were the cause it would have to narrow where the search fails.",
      "L5 has the WIDEST basin measured (5) and a solve rate of 0.000. The ordering is the opposite of what the hypothesis requires.",
      "Partial credit EXISTS at every rung: one mutation from a solution still beats a random tree, ratios 0.605 to 0.914. The claim that L4 offers no partial credit is simply false.",
      "Neutrality is flat at 0.047-0.083 across all six rungs, so a plateau difference does not distinguish them either."
    ],
    "what_this_rules_out": "Local landscape shape around the solution -- basin radius, partial credit, and neutrality -- is NOT what separates the solvable rungs from the unsolvable ones. Since eight operators also fail identically, the cause is neither the operator nor the basin's local geometry.",
    "what_remains": "The basin radius is roughly constant at about 4 mutations while the search space grows with target size, so the live hypothesis is now about how hard the basin is to FIND rather than how it is shaped: solution density, or the number of distinct trees realising each target, falling as the target grows. That is a different measurement and is not attempted here.",
    "the_gradient_is_weak_everywhere_including_where_search_succeeds": "At k=1 the perturbed median is only 10-40% better than random even at L1 and L2, which solve at 0.750 and 0.969. So a weak gradient is not the discriminator either -- the easy rungs have one too.",
    "not_established": [
      "300 samples per cell, and the estimates are visibly noisy: L3's k=5 median reads as informative (0.944) after k=4 read as no-signal (1.000), which is non-monotone and cannot be real. Basin width takes the FIRST failing k, so the widths stand, but they are integers estimated from noisy medians and should not be read to a finer resolution than +/- 1.",
      "The random medians differ across rungs by a factor of 7 (78.5 to 569), so the k=1 RATIOS are comparable within a rung but the absolute fitness scales are not comparable across rungs. Basin width is scale-free because it is a within-rung comparison, which is why the conclusion rests on it rather than on the ratios.",
      "Basin width is measured along the mutation operator's own moves, so it describes the landscape as that operator sees it."
    ]
  }
}
